 "lore": [
  "The Izan say their first word was iz, tree, and that every other word grew from it the way branches grow from a stem. So their letters are stems with branches, cut with a knife along the grain. Every child’s first carving is iz: one short stroke and one stem with two falling branches.",
  "They do not say the city was built. They say it was planted: izak, not salak. The first house stood beside the first tree; that tree, Izor, still stands on the hill below the Salor, the great house, which came later. The city grew outward from its shade. The full name, Izisa, still means \"tree-house\". The old trees inside the walls (saliz, noriz, vaeliz, shaeliz) are never cut. Iziz iz, \"the forest thrives\", is cut over the inside of every gate.",
  "Three gates open on the world. Vaedun, the Water Gate, faces south towards Vaeren and the lake. Shaedun, the Star Gate, faces west, towards the evening sky. Seldun, the Hill Gate, faces north-east, under the palace hill. Their doors drop at night, and the watchmen, halan, are posted (halak) at dawn and dusk.",
  "Izani is blunt, and so are its walls. A sign is one word: the place. A bakery is muneth, bread-place; a smithy is koreth, iron-place. There are no signs that ask or warn, because there is no word for please. The sewer outfall is simply Dusren, filth-flow.",
  "Paint is for the young. Soldiers’ children write thur un mae, \"war: no good\", on the wall walks. Lovers paint two names side by side. Girls are often named for small bright things, like Shaelis, firefly; boys for great ones, like Izor, great tree.",
  "Above the city: Sor, the red dwarf; Nu, the green moon, which waxes and wanes over eight days; Nuis, the pale moon, which crosses the sky every twenty hours. The carvers’ constellations are Kalir the Bridge, Fenis the Lantern, Ush the Serpent, Tor the Crown and Lolo the Twins.",
  "Every third evening is Yolshae, the Feast of Lights, with fenshae (fire-stars) over the Shaesa. On feast days the boatmen race, naethur, on Shaevae, the star-water, so called because it holds the stars at night. Its water runs out as Vaeren, and at the falls the carvers wrote vaeren vae: \"the river flows\", which is how the Izan say that time passes.",
  "Trade comes by Naeten, the boat road, from Naeleth, the docks, to the Water Gate. Travellers, tenan, sleep at Dometh halfway along. The moat around the city is Thurvae, war-water, though no one alive remembers it being used for war."
 ],
 "canon": [
  "Iziz is a forest world orbiting a red dwarf. Its light is low and red; the forest covers almost everything, and the city grew up inside it rather than in a clearing cut from it."
 ]
}